<script>
import DealShortcuts from 'crm/src/deals/lib/DealShortcuts.abide'
import dealShortcutsSource from 'crm/src/deals/lib/DealShortcuts.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import dealShortcutsTestSource from 'crm/src/deals/lib/DealShortcuts.test.ts' with { type: 'text' }
import SaveShortcut from 'crm/src/deals/lib/SaveShortcut.abide'
import saveShortcutSource from 'crm/src/deals/lib/SaveShortcut.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import saveShortcutTestSource from 'crm/src/deals/lib/SaveShortcut.test.ts' with { type: 'text' }

import Doc from '#src/lib/Doc.abide'
import Example from '#src/lib/Example.abide'
import Registry from '#src/lib/Registry.abide'
import Section from '#src/lib/Section.abide'
import Snippet from '#src/lib/Snippet.abide'
</script>

<Doc>
    <Section title="A shortcut stands aside while someone types">
        <Example
            id="hotkeys-shortcuts"
            title="`hotkeys(bindings)` — a key to a command, across the page"
            summary="A rep presses n to start a deal, then types its name into the field it opened, every n in it included. ? lists the keys, and Escape closes either, from the field too."
            files={{
                'src/deals/lib/DealShortcuts.abide': dealShortcutsSource,
                'src/deals/lib/DealShortcuts.test.ts': dealShortcutsTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <DealShortcuts />
        </Example>

        <p><code>hotkeys</code> hands back one <code>keydown</code> listener for every binding.
            Put it on <code>{'<window onkeydown>'}</code> and it hears the whole page, and it
            leaves with the component that put it there. Put it on an element and it hears only
            the keys pressed inside that element.</p>

        <Snippet source={dealShortcutsSource} path="src/deals/lib/DealShortcuts.abide"
            from={['const keys = hotkeys(']} />

        <p>A press in a text field, a textarea, a select or an editable element is the visitor's,
            so a bare <code>n</code> never runs there. A binding written as
            <code>{'{ run, editable: true }'}</code> runs there too, the way Escape closes the
            field it was pressed in. <code>isEditableTarget</code> is the same test, for a
            listener of your own.</p>

        <p>The binding that runs calls <code>preventDefault</code>, so the <code>/</code> that
            focuses a search box is not typed into it. A press another handler already took is
            skipped: a menu that closes on Escape and calls <code>preventDefault</code> keeps the
            page's Escape from closing what is under it. A key an input method is composing with
            is never a shortcut.</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>You write</th><th>What it matches</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>'n'</code></td><td>the n key with no modifier, and not shift+n</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>'shift+n'</code></td><td>N, typed with shift</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>'?'</code></td><td>whatever types <code>?</code>, shift included, since a symbol is matched by what it types</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>'ctrl+k'</code>, <code>'meta+k'</code>, <code>'alt+k'</code></td><td>that modifier alone, on every platform</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>'Escape'</code>, <code>'ArrowUp'</code>, <code>'space'</code></td><td>a named key as <code>KeyboardEvent.key</code> names it, matched case-blind</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>A binding is matched by the character it types, so it follows the visitor's keyboard
            layout. Alt composes a letter into another character on a Mac, so
            <code>alt+k</code> is matched by where the key sits instead. A binding naming a
            modifier that is not one of the five throws as <code>hotkeys</code> reads it.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="`mod` is ⌘ on a Mac and Ctrl elsewhere, in the key and in its label">
        <Example
            id="hotkeys-mod"
            title="`'mod+Enter'` and `keyLabel('mod+Enter')` — one binding, two platforms"
            summary="A rep writes a note on the Ledger audit deal and saves it without leaving the textarea. The button's hint reads ⌘Enter on their Mac and Ctrl+Enter on a colleague's Windows laptop."
            files={{
                'src/deals/lib/SaveShortcut.abide': saveShortcutSource,
                'src/deals/lib/SaveShortcut.test.ts': saveShortcutTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <SaveShortcut />
        </Example>

        <p><code>mod</code> is the modifier a platform saves and copies with: ⌘ on a Mac, an
            iPhone or an iPad, and Ctrl anywhere else. <code>keyLabel</code> writes a binding the
            way that platform writes it, so a hint never names a key the binding does not take.</p>

        <Snippet source={saveShortcutSource} path="src/deals/lib/SaveShortcut.abide"
            from={['const keys = hotkeys(', '<kbd']} />

        <p>Both read the platform off the user agent: the request's on the server and the
            browser's on the page. The label the server renders is the one the page hydrates, so
            nothing swaps after load.</p>
    </Section>

    <Registry />
</Doc>
